--- Rosetta Code: Narcissist
--- https://rosettacode.org/wiki/Narcissist
--- A narcissist in the manner of a quine: the program does not read its own file
--- but rebuilds its source from the template t below, in which a tilde stands for
--- a line break, a hash for a quote and an at sign for the template itself (those
--- three characters, a backtick and a backslash appear nowhere else in the
--- source). The candidate is the whole of standard input, read as one text, so
--- any finite input is compared character for character; the answer is accept or
--- reject. (narcissist.in holds a copy of this file, so the run prints accept.)
use bits

fn source_of[t]
    --- the program text that the template t stands for
    q = char[39]
    lines = replace[t, char[126], char[10]]
    quoted = replace[lines, char[35], q]
    return replace[quoted, char[64], concat[[q, t, q]]]

t = '--- Rosetta Code: Narcissist~--- https://rosettacode.org/wiki/Narcissist~--- A narcissist in the manner of a quine: the program does not read its own file~--- but rebuilds its source from the template t below, in which a tilde stands for~--- a line break, a hash for a quote and an at sign for the template itself (those~--- three characters, a backtick and a backslash appear nowhere else in the~--- source). The candidate is the whole of standard input, read as one text, so~--- any finite input is compared character for character; the answer is accept or~--- reject. (narcissist.in holds a copy of this file, so the run prints accept.)~use bits~~fn source_of[t]~    --- the program text that the template t stands for~    q = char[39]~    lines = replace[t, char[126], char[10]]~    quoted = replace[lines, char[35], q]~    return replace[quoted, char[64], concat[[q, t, q]]]~~t = @~given = read[#/dev/stdin#]~if given.ok and given eq source_of[t]~    log[#accept#]~else~    log[#reject#]~'
given = read['/dev/stdin']
if given.ok and given eq source_of[t]
    log['accept']
else
    log['reject']
